<div class="page">
  <h1>Organization and Features of a Typical Online Course</h1>
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 30px;">
    <!-- Left side simulation -->
    <div style="width: 45%; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;">
      <div style="font-weight: bold;">WebCampus Interface Mockup</div>
      <div style="margin-top: 10px;">
        <p>Tabs: My FDU | Courses | Student Services | FDU Community</p>
        <div style="margin-top: 15px;">
          <p>Announcements</p>
          <p>Faculty Information</p>
          <p>Course Information</p>
          <p>Course Documents</p>
          <p>Related Resources</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <!-- Right side simulation -->
    <div style="width: 45%; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; text-align: right;">
      <div style="font-weight: bold;">Blackboard Interface Mockup</div>
      <div style="margin-top: 10px;">
        <p>Announcements</p>
        <div style="border: 2px solid red; padding: 10px; text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">
          Access the main "content areas" of the course from the main menu.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> The following is provided as introductory information on the main content areas and features of a typical online course. Online course content and organization will vary.</p>

  <h2>Announcements</h2>
  <p>The "home page" of the class and the area where messages directed at the entire class are posted on a regular basis.</p>

  <h2>Faculty Information</h2>
  <p>Instructor biography, contact information, availability for "virtual" office hours (optional), and general policies regarding instructor availability.</p>

  <h2>Course Information</h2>
  <p>Course syllabus, course calendar, and other general class policy information.</p>

  <h2>Course Documents</h2>
  <p>Contains the majority of the content of the class. Within "Course Documents", course content is organized into folders by instructional "unit". Generally, each unit is designed to be completed within a week's time.</p>

  <h2>Related Resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Blackboard Learn: "<span data-field="submit_assignments_link">{{ submit_assignments_link }}</span>"</li>
    <li>Video: "<span data-field="submit_assignment_video">{{ submit_assignment_video }}</span>" (YouTube, 0:56)</li>
    <li>Video: "<span data-field="content_editor_video">{{ content_editor_video }}</span>" (YouTube, :58)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h1>Discussions</h1>
  <p>Asynchronous discussion forums are where much of the weekly interaction between you and your instructor and fellow students will take place. <strong>Note that, in most cases, anything posted in a discussion forum will be visible to everyone in the class.</strong></p>

  <h2>Related Resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Blackboard Learn: "<span data-field="discussions_link">{{ discussions_link }}</span>"</li>
    <li>Blackboard Video: "<span data-field="discussions_video">{{ discussions_video }}</span>" (YouTube, 2:35)</li>
  </ul>

  <h1>E-mail</h1>
  <p>WebCampus provides an integrated E-mail system for direct communication with your instructor and fellow students. Note that all E-mail correspondences should be conducted with WebCampus and / or Webmail accounts.</p>

  <h2>Related Resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Blackboard Learn: E-mail</li>
    <li>Blackboard video: "<span data-field="send_email_video">{{ send_email_video }}</span>" (YouTube, 1:52)</li>
  </ul>

  <h1>My Grades</h1>
  <p>Instructors may use the WebCampus Gradebook to post student grades and related feedback. "My Grades" provides students with access to assessment feedback according to policies established by the instructor.</p>

  <h2>Related Resources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Blackboard Help: "Grades"</li>
    <li>Video: "<span data-field="check_grades_video">{{ check_grades_video }}</span>" (YouTube, 0:58)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Order Books from the FDU Bookstore</h2>
  <p>Obtain the required textbook information listed by your instructor in your WebCampus course. You can purchase traditional and e-books from the FDU Metropolitan Campus Bookstore via phone at 201-836-7818, or by visiting the <a href="https://www.bkstr.com/fdustore">FDU Bookstore</a> online.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Click on the Textbooks option at the top of the page;</li>
    <li>Select the campus and term from the drop-down list;</li>
    <li>Select Your Program. For example, MADS, CHSA, MSA, etc.; then select the course number and the section.</li>
    <li>Click on "Find Materials for course(s)"</li>
  </ul>
</div>

